  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nLittle will change with the weather pattern for the rest of this\nweek and likely well into next week as the high pressure ridge\nis expected to remain centered over the Desert Southwest. The\ncenter of the ridge is forecast to somewhat shift farther to the\nnorth by Friday which could help to bring better rain chances, but\nguidance shows the ridge also strengthening which will likely\nreinforce the subsidence over much of our area. As of now, Thursday\nand Friday are expected to follow the same pattern as today and\nWednesday with mostly eastern Arizona high terrain storms and very\nconditional 5-15% PoPs into the south-central Arizona lower\ndeserts. As stated in the short-term discussion, even though it\nwill be a day-to-day low chance environment for the lower \ndeserts, it would not be surprising to have a day where the \nelevated instability is managed to be tapped into causing some \nscattered storms to fire. Hot and humid conditions will continue \nto prevail with daytime highs likely nudging upward again late \nweek as the ridge restrengthens. The Extreme Heat Warning \ncurrently goes through Friday which is very reasonable, but it \nwill be possible to have a day in there where it fails to reach \ncriteria over south-central Arizona if convection ends up being \nmore widespread one evening/overnight.\n\nFor the weekend into early next week, ensembles still favor the\nridge basically staying stationary with the center somewhere just\nto our north. Some weakening of the ridge is likely to occur by\nearly next week and depending on how much moisture will be\navailable we could be looking at a further uptick in monsoon \nactivity. The GEFS is more optimistic with rain chances by early \nnext week with activity expanding into central and northern \nArizona, but the EPS holds off until later next week. If \nconvection does eventually become more widespread and impacts the \nlower deserts, temperatures are expected to dip closer to seasonal\nnormals.",
